    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d456">

                <pb facs="1076" n="1048" xml:id="pg1048"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">Eisenhower Library, Dulles papers, “Meetings with
                    the President”</note>
                <head>No. 456<lb/><hi rendition="#italic">Memorandum of Conversation, by the
                        Secretary of State</hi><anchor n="1" xml:id="d456-f1"/></head>
                <opener>
                    <seg rendition="#float-left"><hi rendition="#small-caps">top secret<lb/>personal
                            and private</hi></seg>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">[<placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Washington</hi></placeName>,] <date calendar="gregorian"
                            type="conversation-or-meeting-date" when="1954-12-22">December 22,
                            1954</date>.</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p rendition="#center"><hi rendition="#small-caps">Memorandum of Conversation With
                        the President</hi></p>
                <p>[Here follows discussion not pertaining to China.]</p>
                <p>3. We talked about the China situation and long-range prospects. I emphasized
                    that, in my opinion, the possibilities of change which would be in the interest
                    of the United States would come from either (a) the traditional tendency of the
                    Chinese to be individualistic or (b) the traditional Chinese dislike of
                    foreigners which was bound in the long run to impair relations with Russia. The
                    President said that, under present conditions, no change of our attitude was
                    possible, but that if the Chinese Communists met certain quite obvious
                    requirements, then the situation might be different.<anchor n="2"
                        xml:id="d456-f2"/></p>
                <p>[Here follows further discussion not pertaining to China.]</p>
                <p>6. The President agreed that Chase should stay on for the time being in Taipei,
                    although not necessarily promoted. He asked me to advise Secretary Wilson
                    accordingly.</p>
                <closer><signed>JFD</signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
    <standOff>
        <listAnnotation>
            <note corresp="#d456-f1" n="1" type="source" xml:id="d456-f1-content">
